## Solution

```js
function twoSum(nums, target) {
  const seen = new Map();          // value → index

  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
    const complement = target - nums[i];

    if (seen.has(complement)) return [seen.get(complement), i];

    seen.set(nums[i], i);          // set AFTER checking, so we don't reuse the same element
  }

  return [];
}

twoSum([2, 7, 11, 15], 9);   // [0, 1]
```

**Time O(n), space O(n).**

---

## Why the order matters

Setting before checking would let `[3]` with target `6` return `[0, 0]` — the same element used twice. Check first, then store.

---

## The pattern

This is the **complement lookup** pattern, and it generalises far beyond this problem:

> Whenever you are searching for a pair with a known relationship, store what you have seen in a hash map and look up what you need. It turns O(n²) into O(n).

Same idea appears in: subarray sum equals K, contains duplicate, longest consecutive sequence, and pair with a given difference.

---

## Variant: sorted input

If the array is already sorted, two pointers use **O(1) space**:

```js
function twoSumSorted(nums, target) {
  let left = 0, right = nums.length - 1;

  while (left < right) {
    const sum = nums[left] + nums[right];
    if (sum === target) return [left, right];
    sum < target ? left++ : right--;      // sum too small → raise the floor
  }
  return [];
}
```

Mentioning this trade-off — hash map for unsorted, two pointers for sorted — is what an interviewer is listening for.

---

## Variant: three sum

Sort, then fix one element and two-pointer the rest. O(n²), which is the accepted optimum:

```js
function threeSum(nums) {
  nums.sort((a, b) => a - b);
  const result = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length - 2; i++) {
    if (i > 0 && nums[i] === nums[i - 1]) continue;   // skip duplicates

    let left = i + 1, right = nums.length - 1;
    while (left < right) {
      const sum = nums[i] + nums[left] + nums[right];
      if (sum === 0) {
        result.push([nums[i], nums[left], nums[right]]);
        while (nums[left] === nums[left + 1]) left++;
        while (nums[right] === nums[right - 1]) right--;
        left++; right--;
      } else if (sum < 0) left++;
      else right--;
    }
  }
  return result;
}
```

The duplicate-skipping is where most candidates lose marks.

## Solution

```js
function lengthOfLongestSubstring(s) {
  const lastSeen = new Map();     // char → most recent index
  let left = 0;
  let best = 0;

  for (let right = 0; right < s.length; right++) {
    const char = s[right];

    // Only move left forward, never backward
    if (lastSeen.has(char) && lastSeen.get(char) >= left) {
      left = lastSeen.get(char) + 1;
    }

    lastSeen.set(char, right);
    best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
  }

  return best;
}

lengthOfLongestSubstring('abcabcbb');   // 3  ("abc")
lengthOfLongestSubstring('bbbbb');      // 1  ("b")
lengthOfLongestSubstring('pwwkew');     // 3  ("wke")
```

**Time O(n), space O(min(n, alphabet)).**

---

## The detail that breaks naive versions

```js
if (lastSeen.has(char) && lastSeen.get(char) >= left)
```

The `>= left` guard is essential. Without it, on `"abba"`:

```text
right=3, char='a', lastSeen('a')=0
Without the guard: left jumps BACK to 1, but left is already 2 → window corrupts
```

The left pointer must be monotonic. This is the classic bug in this problem.

---

## The sliding window pattern

```text
Expand right → while the window is invalid, shrink from left → record the best
```

It turns "check every substring" (O(n²) or O(n³)) into O(n) because each pointer only ever moves forward — total movement is 2n.

Applies to: minimum window substring, longest repeating character replacement, maximum sum subarray of size k, fruit into baskets, permutation in string.

---

## The fixed-size variant

When the window size is given, it is even simpler — add the entering element, remove the leaving one:

```js
function maxSumSubarray(nums, k) {
  let sum = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) sum += nums[i];

  let best = sum;
  for (let i = k; i < nums.length; i++) {
    sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];      // slide, don't recompute
    best = Math.max(best, sum);
  }
  return best;
}
```

Recomputing the sum inside the loop would make it O(n·k). The incremental update is the whole point.

---

## How to talk through it

State the brute force first ("check every substring, O(n²)"), then say what makes it wasteful ("we re-scan characters we already know are unique"), then introduce the window. Interviewers want the reasoning path, not the memorised answer.

## Floyd's cycle detection

```js
function hasCycle(head) {
  let slow = head;
  let fast = head;

  while (fast !== null && fast.next !== null) {
    slow = slow.next;         // one step
    fast = fast.next.next;    // two steps

    if (slow === fast) return true;   // they met → cycle
  }

  return false;               // fast hit the end → no cycle
}
```

**Time O(n), space O(1).**

---

## Why they must meet

Inside a cycle, the fast pointer gains exactly one position on the slow pointer per iteration. The gap shrinks by one each step, so it must eventually reach zero. On a straight list, fast runs off the end first.

The loop condition checks **both** `fast` and `fast.next` because fast takes two steps — missing either check throws on a null dereference.

---

## Finding where the cycle starts

```js
function detectCycleStart(head) {
  let slow = head, fast = head;

  while (fast?.next) {
    slow = slow.next;
    fast = fast.next.next;
    if (slow === fast) {
      // Reset one pointer to head; advance both one step at a time.
      // They meet exactly at the cycle entrance.
      let entry = head;
      while (entry !== slow) {
        entry = entry.next;
        slow = slow.next;
      }
      return entry;
    }
  }
  return null;
}
```

The proof is short enough to sketch: if the distance from head to the cycle start is `a` and from the cycle start to the meeting point is `b`, then the remaining cycle length is `c`, and it works out that `a = c` modulo the cycle length. Hence walking `a` from head and `c` from the meeting point converges at the entrance.

---

## The simpler alternative

```js
function hasCycle(head) {
  const seen = new Set();
  let curr = head;
  while (curr) {
    if (seen.has(curr)) return true;
    seen.add(curr);
    curr = curr.next;
  }
  return false;
}
```

O(n) time but **O(n) space**. Mention it as the obvious solution, then offer Floyd's as the O(1)-space improvement — that sequence demonstrates optimisation rather than memorisation.

---

## Where slow/fast pointers also apply

| Problem | Use |
|---------|-----|
| Find the middle node | Fast reaches the end when slow is at the middle |
| Find the nth node from the end | Offset one pointer by n, advance both |
| Palindrome linked list | Find the middle, reverse the second half |
| Happy number | Cycle detection on the digit-square sequence |

The last one is a nice tell: it is the same algorithm on a numeric sequence rather than a list, and recognising that is a good signal.

## Solution

```js
function isValid(s) {
  const pairs = { ')': '(', ']': '[', '}': '{' };
  const stack = [];

  for (const char of s) {
    if (char === '(' || char === '[' || char === '{') {
      stack.push(char);
    } else if (char in pairs) {
      if (stack.pop() !== pairs[char]) return false;   // wrong type, or empty stack
    }
  }

  return stack.length === 0;   // ← unclosed openers left over
}

isValid('()[]{}');   // true
isValid('(]');       // false
isValid('([)]');     // false — nesting order matters
isValid('(');        // false — the final check catches this
```

**Time O(n), space O(n).**

---

## The two checks people miss

**1. `stack.pop()` on an empty stack returns `undefined`**, which will not equal any opener — so `")"` alone correctly returns false without a separate length check. Nice, but worth stating that you knew it rather than got lucky.

**2. The final `stack.length === 0`.** Without it, `"((("` returns true. This is the most common bug in this problem.

---

## Why a stack is the right structure

Brackets are **last-in-first-out** by definition — the most recently opened bracket must be the next one closed. Any problem with that property wants a stack.

The test that catches wrong answers: `"([)]"` has equal counts of every bracket type, so counting alone says balanced. Only a stack captures the nesting order.

---

## Where the stack pattern shows up

| Problem | Use |
|---------|-----|
| Valid parentheses | This |
| Min stack | Auxiliary stack of minimums |
| Evaluate reverse Polish notation | Push operands, pop on operator |
| Daily temperatures | Monotonic stack |
| Largest rectangle in histogram | Monotonic stack |
| Undo/redo | Two stacks |
| Browser history | Two stacks — back and forward |

That last pair is a genuine frontend application worth mentioning.

---

## The follow-up

> "What if the string also contains other characters?"

The solution above already handles it — anything that is neither an opener nor a key in `pairs` is ignored. That is why the `else if (char in pairs)` check exists rather than a bare `else`.

> "What about the minimum number of insertions to balance it?"

Track unmatched closers as you go and add the leftover openers at the end:

```js
function minInsertions(s) {
  let open = 0, insertions = 0;
  for (const c of s) {
    if (c === '(') open++;
    else if (open > 0) open--;
    else insertions++;          // a closer with nothing to close
  }
  return insertions + open;     // plus every unclosed opener
}
```

## The reduce version

```js
function groupBy(items, keyFn) {
  return items.reduce((acc, item) => {
    const key = keyFn(item);
    (acc[key] ??= []).push(item);      // create the array on first touch
    return acc;
  }, {});
}

groupBy(users, (u) => u.role);
// { admin: [...], editor: [...], viewer: [...] }
```

**O(n) time and space.**

`(acc[key] ??= []).push(item)` is the compact idiom — logical nullish assignment creates the array only if absent, and returns it either way so you can push straight into it.

---

## The built-ins

```js
Object.groupBy(users, (u) => u.role);      // plain object, null prototype
Map.groupBy(users, (u) => u.department);   // Map — keys can be any type
```

Both are now widely supported. `Map.groupBy` matters when the key is an object or you need insertion order guaranteed for non-string keys.

---

## Multi-level grouping

```js
function groupByMulti(items, ...keyFns) {
  if (keyFns.length === 0) return items;

  const [first, ...rest] = keyFns;
  const grouped = groupBy(items, first);

  for (const key of Object.keys(grouped)) {
    grouped[key] = groupByMulti(grouped[key], ...rest);
  }
  return grouped;
}

groupByMulti(sales, (s) => s.year, (s) => s.region);
// { 2025: { EU: [...], US: [...] }, 2026: { … } }
```

---

## The React relevance

This is genuinely common — grouping list items into sections:

```jsx
function Timeline({ events }) {
  // Memoise: this is O(n) on every render otherwise
  const byDay = useMemo(
    () => Object.groupBy(events, (e) => formatDate(e.timestamp)),
    [events]
  );

  return Object.entries(byDay).map(([day, dayEvents]) => (
    <section key={day}>
      <h2>{day}</h2>
      {dayEvents.map((e) => <Event key={e.id} event={e} />)}
    </section>
  ));
}
```

---

## The related pattern: index by id

Grouping's sibling, and arguably more useful in React:

```js
const byId = new Map(users.map((u) => [u.id, u]));
byId.get('u_42');    // O(1) instead of O(n) find
```

```jsx
// ❌ O(n) lookup inside an O(n) render → O(n²)
{postIds.map((id) => <Post key={id} post={posts.find((p) => p.id === id)} />)}

// ✅ Build the index once
const postsById = useMemo(() => new Map(posts.map((p) => [p.id, p])), [posts]);
{postIds.map((id) => <Post key={id} post={postsById.get(id)} />)}
```

That `find`-inside-`map` is a real performance bug that appears constantly in React code, and spotting it is a better answer than the algorithm itself.

## The built-in

```js
[1, [2, [3, [4, [5]]]]].flat(Infinity);   // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
[1, [2, [3, [4]]]].flat(2);                // [1, 2, 3, [4]]

// flatMap = map then flat(1) — useful and often overlooked
posts.flatMap((p) => p.tags);              // all tags, one array
```

Say this first. Then implement it, because that is what they are asking for.

---

## Recursive implementation

```js
function flatten(arr, depth = Infinity) {
  const result = [];

  for (const item of arr) {
    if (Array.isArray(item) && depth > 0) {
      result.push(...flatten(item, depth - 1));
    } else {
      result.push(item);
    }
  }

  return result;
}
```

**O(n) time** where n is the total element count. Space is O(n) plus O(d) call stack for depth d.

> Caution on `push(...spread)`: with a very large sub-array this can exceed the argument limit. `result.push.apply` has the same issue. For huge arrays, a loop or `concat` is safer — a good detail to raise.

---

## Iterative, with an explicit stack

Avoids the recursion depth limit entirely:

```js
function flatten(arr) {
  const stack = [...arr];
  const result = [];

  while (stack.length) {
    const item = stack.pop();

    if (Array.isArray(item)) {
      stack.push(...item);      // push back to process later
    } else {
      result.push(item);
    }
  }

  return result.reverse();      // pop reverses order — undo it
}
```

The `reverse()` at the end is the detail people forget. Using `shift()` instead avoids it but is O(n) per operation.

---

## The general principle

> **Any recursive algorithm can be made iterative with an explicit stack.**

The call stack *is* a stack — you are just managing it yourself. This matters when depth could exceed roughly 10,000 frames, which is where JavaScript engines throw.

---

## The frontend relevance

Flattening a nested comment tree or a menu hierarchy into a flat list is the standard way to make a tree virtualisable:

```js
function flattenTree(nodes, depth = 0, out = []) {
  for (const node of nodes) {
    out.push({ ...node, depth });
    if (node.expanded && node.children?.length) {
      flattenTree(node.children, depth + 1, out);
    }
  }
  return out;
}
```

Carrying `depth` through gives you indentation, and honouring `expanded` means collapsed branches are skipped entirely. Virtualising this flat projection is straightforward; virtualising the tree directly is not.

That connection — flatten to virtualise — is a much stronger answer than the algorithm alone.

## Solution

```js
function firstUniqueChar(s) {
  const counts = new Map();

  for (const char of s) {
    counts.set(char, (counts.get(char) ?? 0) + 1);
  }

  for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {
    if (counts.get(s[i]) === 1) return i;
  }

  return -1;
}

firstUniqueChar('leetcode');    // 0  ('l')
firstUniqueChar('loveleetcode'); // 2  ('v')
firstUniqueChar('aabb');        // -1
```

**Time O(n), space O(k)** where k is the alphabet size.

---

## Using Map's insertion order

```js
function firstUniqueChar(s) {
  const counts = new Map();
  for (const char of s) counts.set(char, (counts.get(char) ?? 0) + 1);

  // Map iterates in insertion order — the first entry with count 1
  // is the first unique character
  for (const [char, count] of counts) {
    if (count === 1) return s.indexOf(char);
  }
  return -1;
}
```

Still two passes, but the second is over the distinct characters rather than the whole string — better when the alphabet is much smaller than the input.

**A plain object would also work here** because string keys iterate in insertion order for non-integer keys, but relying on that is fragile — `{'1': …, 'a': …}` puts `'1'` first regardless of insertion. `Map` has no such rule and is the safer choice. That distinction is a good thing to mention.

---

## The related family

| Problem | Approach |
|---------|----------|
| Valid anagram | Count both, compare the maps |
| Group anagrams | Key by sorted characters, or by a count signature |
| Ransom note | Count the magazine, decrement per note character |
| Character frequency sort | Count, then sort entries by count |
| Find all duplicates | Count, filter count > 1 |

All of them are the same counting-map pattern. Recognising the family is more valuable than the individual solutions.

---

## Anagram, since it always follows

```js
function isAnagram(a, b) {
  if (a.length !== b.length) return false;      // cheap early exit

  const counts = new Map();
  for (const c of a) counts.set(c, (counts.get(c) ?? 0) + 1);

  for (const c of b) {
    const n = counts.get(c);
    if (!n) return false;                        // absent or already exhausted
    counts.set(c, n - 1);
  }

  return true;
}
```

O(n), versus O(n log n) for the sort-and-compare approach. Note the length check first — it is free and eliminates most negative cases immediately.

---

## The Unicode caveat

```js
[...'héllo'].length;      // 5 with a precomposed é
'👨‍👩‍👧'.length;              // 8 — one visible character, eight code units
```

Iterating a string with `for...of` handles code points correctly, unlike indexing. For full grapheme clusters you need `Intl.Segmenter`. Raising this shows care about real-world input.

## Merge into a new array

```js
function merge(a, b) {
  const result = [];
  let i = 0, j = 0;

  while (i < a.length && j < b.length) {
    result.push(a[i] <= b[j] ? a[i++] : b[j++]);   // <= keeps it stable
  }

  // One array is exhausted; append the remainder of the other
  while (i < a.length) result.push(a[i++]);
  while (j < b.length) result.push(b[j++]);

  return result;
}
```

**Time O(n + m), space O(n + m).**

Using `<=` rather than `<` makes the merge **stable** — equal elements keep their relative order, with `a` first. That matters when sorting objects by one field.

---

## In place, from the back

Classic follow-up: `a` has space at the end for `b`'s elements.

```js
function mergeInPlace(a, m, b, n) {
  let i = m - 1;        // last real element of a
  let j = n - 1;        // last element of b
  let k = m + n - 1;    // last slot overall

  while (j >= 0) {
    // Fill from the back — those slots are empty, so nothing is overwritten
    a[k--] = (i >= 0 && a[i] > b[j]) ? a[i--] : b[j--];
  }
}
```

**O(1) extra space.** Filling from the front would overwrite elements of `a` before reading them; from the back, every slot written is one that was empty or already consumed. This back-to-front insight is the entire trick.

The loop only needs `j >= 0` — if `b` is exhausted, `a`'s remaining elements are already in place.

---

## Why this matters beyond the question

This is the merge step of **merge sort**:

```js
function mergeSort(arr) {
  if (arr.length <= 1) return arr;

  const mid = Math.floor(arr.length / 2);
  return merge(mergeSort(arr.slice(0, mid)), mergeSort(arr.slice(mid)));
}
```

**O(n log n) guaranteed** — unlike quicksort's O(n²) worst case — and **stable**, which is why it underlies most language sort implementations. V8 uses TimSort, a hybrid of merge sort and insertion sort.

---

## Merging k sorted arrays

The natural follow-up. Merging pairwise repeatedly is O(k·n). A min-heap of the k current heads is O(n log k):

```text
Push the first element of each array into a heap.
Pop the minimum, append it, push the next element from that same array.
Repeat.
```

JavaScript has no built-in heap, so say you would implement a binary heap or use a library — and note that for small k, repeated pairwise merging is fine and simpler.

---

## Sorting caution in JavaScript

```js
[10, 9, 1].sort();                 // [1, 10, 9] — lexicographic by default!
[10, 9, 1].sort((a, b) => a - b);  // [1, 9, 10]
```

The default comparator converts to strings. This catches people out constantly and is worth mentioning unprompted.

## Kadane's algorithm

```js
function maxSubArray(nums) {
  let currentMax = nums[0];
  let globalMax = nums[0];

  for (let i = 1; i < nums.length; i++) {
    // Extend the previous subarray, or start a new one here
    currentMax = Math.max(nums[i], currentMax + nums[i]);
    globalMax = Math.max(globalMax, currentMax);
  }

  return globalMax;
}

maxSubArray([-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4]);   // 6  → [4, -1, 2, 1]
```

**Time O(n), space O(1).**

---

## The intuition

The decision at each element is binary: is the subarray I have been building **helping or hurting**?

```text
currentMax + nums[i]  vs  nums[i]
```

If `currentMax` is negative, adding it makes things worse than starting fresh. So a negative running sum is discarded — that is the whole algorithm.

---

## Trace it

```text
nums:        -2   1  -3   4  -1   2   1  -5   4
currentMax:  -2   1  -2   4   3   5   6   1   5
globalMax:   -2   1   1   4   4   5   6   6   6
```

At index 1, `currentMax + 1 = -1` is worse than `1`, so it restarts. At index 3, `-2 + 4 = 2` is worse than `4`, so it restarts again — and that restart is where the answer begins.

---

## Initialising with `nums[0]`, not `0`

```js
// ❌ Returns 0 for an all-negative array
let globalMax = 0;

// ✅ Returns -1 for [-3, -1, -2], which is correct
let globalMax = nums[0];
```

If the subarray must be non-empty, an all-negative input must return the least-negative element. Initialising to `0` silently returns `0`, and this is the most common bug on this problem.

---

## Returning the indices

```js
function maxSubArrayWithRange(nums) {
  let currentMax = nums[0], globalMax = nums[0];
  let start = 0, bestStart = 0, bestEnd = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i < nums.length; i++) {
    if (nums[i] > currentMax + nums[i]) {
      currentMax = nums[i];
      start = i;                    // restarted here
    } else {
      currentMax += nums[i];
    }

    if (currentMax > globalMax) {
      globalMax = currentMax;
      bestStart = start;
      bestEnd = i;
    }
  }

  return { sum: globalMax, start: bestStart, end: bestEnd };
}
```

---

## The family

| Problem | Variation |
|---------|-----------|
| Maximum product subarray | Track min *and* max — a negative flips them |
| Best time to buy and sell stock | Kadane on the differences |
| Circular maximum subarray | Answer is either normal Kadane, or total minus minimum subarray |
| Maximum sum with at most one deletion | Two running states |

The stock problem is the same algorithm in disguise — recognising that is a strong signal.

---

## What this is really testing

Whether you can find the **optimal substructure**: the best subarray ending at `i` depends only on the best ending at `i-1`. That is the seed of dynamic programming, and Kadane is usually the first DP problem people meet.

## Depth-first, recursive

```js
function inorder(node, out = []) {
  if (!node) return out;
  inorder(node.left, out);
  out.push(node.value);        // visit BETWEEN children
  inorder(node.right, out);
  return out;
}

function preorder(node, out = []) {
  if (!node) return out;
  out.push(node.value);        // visit BEFORE children
  preorder(node.left, out);
  preorder(node.right, out);
  return out;
}

function postorder(node, out = []) {
  if (!node) return out;
  postorder(node.left, out);
  postorder(node.right, out);
  out.push(node.value);        // visit AFTER children
  return out;
}
```

The three are identical except for **where the push sits**. That is the whole distinction, and it is worth saying explicitly.

**Inorder on a binary search tree yields sorted order** — the most useful property to remember.

---

## Breadth-first, with a queue

```js
function levelOrder(root) {
  if (!root) return [];

  const levels = [];
  const queue = [root];

  while (queue.length) {
    const levelSize = queue.length;    // ← snapshot before adding children
    const level = [];

    for (let i = 0; i < levelSize; i++) {
      const node = queue.shift();
      level.push(node.value);
      if (node.left) queue.push(node.left);
      if (node.right) queue.push(node.right);
    }

    levels.push(level);
  }

  return levels;
}
```

Capturing `levelSize` before the inner loop is what separates the levels. Without it you get a flat list.

> `shift()` is O(n) on a JavaScript array. For large trees, use an index pointer instead of shifting, or a proper deque.

---

## Choosing

| Use DFS | Use BFS |
|---------|---------|
| Explore a whole branch | Shortest path in an unweighted graph |
| Path-sum problems | Level-by-level processing |
| Tree height, validation | Nearest node matching a condition |
| Lower memory on deep trees | Lower memory on wide trees |

Memory is the practical trade-off: DFS is O(height), BFS is O(width). A deep skinny tree favours BFS; a wide shallow one favours DFS.

---

## Iterative DFS

```js
function preorderIterative(root) {
  if (!root) return [];
  const stack = [root], out = [];

  while (stack.length) {
    const node = stack.pop();
    out.push(node.value);
    // Right first, so left is popped first
    if (node.right) stack.push(node.right);
    if (node.left) stack.push(node.left);
  }
  return out;
}
```

Avoids stack overflow on very deep trees. The push order being reversed is the detail to explain.

