## The React connection

React's reconciler walks the Fiber tree depth-first, and Fiber replaced the recursive implementation with an iterative one using explicit parent/child/sibling pointers **precisely so rendering can be paused and resumed** — you cannot pause a call stack. That is why concurrent rendering was possible only after the Fiber rewrite.

Making that link turns a generic tree question into a React answer.

## The JavaScript shortcut

```js
class LRUCache {
  #cache = new Map();

  constructor(capacity) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
  }

  get(key) {
    if (!this.#cache.has(key)) return -1;

    const value = this.#cache.get(key);
    this.#cache.delete(key);
    this.#cache.set(key, value);       // re-insert → now most recent
    return value;
  }

  put(key, value) {
    this.#cache.delete(key);           // remove if present, so re-set moves it
    this.#cache.set(key, value);

    if (this.#cache.size > this.capacity) {
      // keys() yields in insertion order — the first is least recently used
      this.#cache.delete(this.#cache.keys().next().value);
    }
  }
}
```

**O(1) for both operations.** `Map` guarantees insertion order, and `delete` plus `set` is the reordering primitive.

Say this version first — it is idiomatic JavaScript. Then offer the classic implementation, because that is usually what is being asked for.

