## The three details interviewers check

**1. Why doubly linked?** Removal needs the previous node. A singly linked list would require an O(n) scan to find it.

**2. Why sentinel head and tail?** They eliminate every empty-list and single-element special case. Without them the code doubles in size and gains three bugs.

**3. Why store the key on the node?** On eviction you have the node but need to delete from the map, which is keyed by key. Forgetting this is the most common mistake.

---

## Where it matters in frontend

Bounded memoisation caches, image caches in a gallery, and query caches all need eviction. An unbounded cache is a memory leak with extra steps — which is the practical takeaway worth stating.

## Subsets — include or exclude

```js
function subsets(nums) {
  const result = [];
  const current = [];

  function backtrack(start) {
    result.push([...current]);        // every prefix is a valid subset — copy it

    for (let i = start; i < nums.length; i++) {
      current.push(nums[i]);          // choose
      backtrack(i + 1);               // recurse on what remains
      current.pop();                  // un-choose ← the backtrack
    }
  }

  backtrack(0);
  return result;
}

subsets([1, 2, 3]);
// [[], [1], [1,2], [1,2,3], [1,3], [2], [2,3], [3]]
```

**O(2ⁿ) results, O(n·2ⁿ) total work.**

---

## Permutations — track what is used

```js
function permute(nums) {
  const result = [];
  const current = [];
  const used = new Array(nums.length).fill(false);

  function backtrack() {
    if (current.length === nums.length) {
      result.push([...current]);      // ← copy, not the reference
      return;
    }

    for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
      if (used[i]) continue;

      used[i] = true;
      current.push(nums[i]);

      backtrack();

      current.pop();                  // undo
      used[i] = false;                // undo
    }
  }

  backtrack();
  return result;
}
```

**O(n!) results.**

---

## The two mistakes

**1. Pushing the reference instead of a copy.**

```js
result.push(current);      // ❌ every entry points at the same array,
                           //    which is empty by the time you return
result.push([...current]); // ✅
```

**2. Forgetting to undo.** Every mutation before the recursive call needs a matching undo after it. Miss one and later branches inherit stale state — and the bug is very hard to spot in the output.

