## The honest note

Exponential output means these only work for small n — around 10 for permutations, 20 for subsets. If the input is large, the intended solution is dynamic programming or a greedy approach, not enumeration. Recognising that saves you from optimising an approach that cannot work.

## Climbing stairs

> You can climb 1 or 2 steps at a time. How many ways to reach step n?

```js
// Bottom-up, O(n) time, O(1) space
function climbStairs(n) {
  if (n <= 2) return n;

  let prev2 = 1, prev1 = 2;
  for (let i = 3; i <= n; i++) {
    [prev2, prev1] = [prev1, prev1 + prev2];
  }
  return prev1;
}
```

The recurrence: to reach step `n` you came from `n-1` or `n-2`, so `ways(n) = ways(n-1) + ways(n-2)`. That is Fibonacci.

The naive recursion is O(2ⁿ) because it recomputes the same subproblems exponentially often — which is exactly the signal that DP applies.

