## Implementation

```js
class Queue {
  #input = [];
  #output = [];

  enqueue(value) {
    this.#input.push(value);          // always O(1)
  }

  dequeue() {
    this.#transfer();
    return this.#output.pop();
  }

  peek() {
    this.#transfer();
    return this.#output.at(-1);
  }

  #transfer() {
    // ONLY when output is empty — this is what makes it amortised O(1)
    if (this.#output.length === 0) {
      while (this.#input.length) {
        this.#output.push(this.#input.pop());
      }
    }
  }

  get size() { return this.#input.length + this.#output.length; }
  get isEmpty() { return this.size === 0; }
}
```

---

## Why it works

```text
enqueue 1, 2, 3   → input: [1, 2, 3]     output: []

dequeue           → transfer (pop from input, push to output)
                  → input: []            output: [3, 2, 1]
                  → pop() returns 1      ✅ first in, first out

dequeue           → output not empty, no transfer
                  → pop() returns 2      ✅
```

A stack reverses order. Two stacks reverse it twice, which restores the original order — that is the entire insight.

---

## The amortised analysis

This is the part interviewers are testing.

A single `dequeue` that triggers a transfer is O(n). But **each element is moved from input to output exactly once in its lifetime**. Over n operations the total work is O(n), so the average per operation is O(1).

The critical condition is transferring **only when output is empty**. Transferring on every dequeue would be genuinely O(n) each time:

```js
// ❌ Breaks the amortised guarantee
#transfer() {
  while (this.#input.length) this.#output.push(this.#input.pop());
}
```

