```js
// Simple, but shift() is O(n) — it reindexes the entire array
const queue = [];
queue.push(x);
queue.shift();
```

For a few hundred items this is fine. For a BFS over a large graph it is a real bottleneck. The alternative without two stacks is an index pointer:

```js
let head = 0;
const queue = [];
function dequeue() { return queue[head++]; }   // O(1), but memory grows
```

Simplest and fastest, at the cost of never reclaiming the consumed prefix until you compact it.

