## Where it shows up in frontend

| Situation | Cycle risk |
|-----------|-----------|
| `JSON.stringify` on a graph | Throws on circular structures |
| Deep clone / deep equal | Infinite recursion |
| Module imports | Bundler circular-dependency warning |
| React `useEffect` setting its own dependency | Infinite render loop — the same shape |
| Component tree with a shared parent reference | Infinite render |

That React row is worth mentioning: an effect that sets state it also depends on is a cycle in the dependency graph, and it fails the same way.

## Implementation

```js
function deepEqual(a, b, seen = new WeakMap()) {
  // Fast path — also handles NaN correctly, unlike ===
  if (Object.is(a, b)) return true;

  // If either is not an object, Object.is was the final word
  if (a === null || b === null) return false;
  if (typeof a !== 'object' || typeof b !== 'object') return false;

  // Different constructors → not equal (Array vs Object, etc.)
  if (Object.getPrototypeOf(a) !== Object.getPrototypeOf(b)) return false;

  // Cycle guard
  if (seen.get(a) === b) return true;
  seen.set(a, b);

  // Built-ins with their own semantics
  if (a instanceof Date) return a.getTime() === b.getTime();
  if (a instanceof RegExp) return a.source === b.source && a.flags === b.flags;

  if (a instanceof Map) {
    if (a.size !== b.size) return false;
    for (const [key, val] of a) {
      if (!b.has(key) || !deepEqual(val, b.get(key), seen)) return false;
    }
    return true;
  }

  if (a instanceof Set) {
    if (a.size !== b.size) return false;
    for (const val of a) if (!b.has(val)) return false;   // shallow for Sets
    return true;
  }

  if (Array.isArray(a)) {
    if (a.length !== b.length) return false;
    return a.every((val, i) => deepEqual(val, b[i], seen));
  }

  const keysA = Reflect.ownKeys(a);
  const keysB = Reflect.ownKeys(b);
  if (keysA.length !== keysB.length) return false;

  return keysA.every(
    (key) => Object.hasOwn(b, key) && deepEqual(a[key], b[key], seen)
  );
}
```

---

## The cases people miss

| Case | Naive result | Correct |
|------|--------------|---------|
| `NaN` vs `NaN` | `false` with `===` | `true` — use `Object.is` |
| `new Date(0)` vs `new Date(0)` | `false` — different refs | `true` — compare `getTime()` |
| `[1,2]` vs `{0:1, 1:2}` | Same keys and values | Different types |
| Cyclic object | Stack overflow | `WeakMap` guard |
| `{a: undefined}` vs `{}` | Same by naive key loop | Different — key counts differ |
| Symbol keys | Missed by `Object.keys` | `Reflect.ownKeys` |

The key-count check catches `{a: 1}` vs `{a: 1, b: 2}` — comparing only `a`'s keys would wrongly report equal.

