## Why React uses shallow comparison instead

```jsx
// React compares by reference — Object.is, O(1)
useEffect(() => { … }, [config]);
```

A deep comparison on every render, on every dependency, would frequently cost more than the re-render it avoids — and it is unbounded for large state.

So the guidance stands: **depend on primitives, or stabilise the reference with `useMemo`.** Reaching for a `useDeepCompareEffect` hook is usually a sign the data shape is wrong.

```jsx
// ❌ Deep comparison per render
useDeepCompareEffect(() => { … }, [config]);

// ✅ Stable reference, O(1) comparison
const config = useMemo(() => ({ limit, sort }), [limit, sort]);
useEffect(() => { … }, [config]);
```

In production, use `fast-deep-equal` or lodash's `isEqual` rather than hand-rolling — but be able to write it, because the edge cases are the question.

## Solution

```js
function numIslands(grid) {
  if (!grid?.length) return 0;

  const rows = grid.length, cols = grid[0].length;
  let count = 0;

  function sink(r, c) {
    // Out of bounds, or water/already visited
    if (r < 0 || r >= rows || c < 0 || c >= cols || grid[r][c] !== '1') return;

    grid[r][c] = '0';        // ← mark visited IMMEDIATELY

    sink(r + 1, c);
    sink(r - 1, c);
    sink(r, c + 1);
    sink(r, c - 1);
  }

  for (let r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
    for (let c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
      if (grid[r][c] === '1') {
        count++;             // a new island
        sink(r, c);          // erase all of it
      }
    }
  }

  return count;
}
```

**Time O(rows × cols)** — every cell is visited at most once. **Space O(rows × cols)** worst case for the recursion stack.

---

## The critical detail

Mark the cell as visited **before** recursing, not after. Marking after means the four recursive calls can bounce back into a cell already being processed, causing infinite recursion.

Mutating the grid to mark visited is the neat trick here — no extra data structure. If mutating the input is not allowed, use a separate `visited` Set of `"r,c"` strings, and say why you switched.

---

## Iterative BFS, to avoid stack overflow

```js
function sinkBFS(grid, startR, startC) {
  const queue = [[startR, startC]];
  grid[startR][startC] = '0';

  const directions = [[1,0], [-1,0], [0,1], [0,-1]];

  for (let i = 0; i < queue.length; i++) {   // index pointer — avoids O(n) shift()
    const [r, c] = queue[i];

    for (const [dr, dc] of directions) {
      const nr = r + dr, nc = c + dc;
      if (nr >= 0 && nr < grid.length && nc >= 0 && nc < grid[0].length
          && grid[nr][nc] === '1') {
        grid[nr][nc] = '0';      // mark on ENQUEUE, not dequeue
        queue.push([nr, nc]);
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Marking on enqueue rather than dequeue prevents the same cell being added to the queue multiple times — the standard BFS optimisation.

On a 1000×1000 grid the recursive version can exceed the call stack, so the iterative version is the safer production choice.

