## Where grids appear in frontend

Spreadsheet cell dependency regions, canvas paint-bucket fill, tile-based game maps, and connected-component selection in a diagram editor. The paint bucket in any drawing tool is literally this algorithm.

## Kahn's algorithm — the intuitive one

```js
function taskOrder(tasks, dependencies) {
  // Build the graph and count incoming edges
  const graph = new Map(tasks.map((t) => [t, []]));
  const inDegree = new Map(tasks.map((t) => [t, 0]));

  for (const [task, dependsOn] of dependencies) {
    graph.get(dependsOn).push(task);            // dependsOn → task
    inDegree.set(task, inDegree.get(task) + 1);
  }

  // Everything with no remaining dependencies can start now
  const queue = tasks.filter((t) => inDegree.get(t) === 0);
  const order = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < queue.length; i++) {
    const task = queue[i];
    order.push(task);

    for (const next of graph.get(task)) {
      inDegree.set(next, inDegree.get(next) - 1);
      if (inDegree.get(next) === 0) queue.push(next);   // now unblocked
    }
  }

  // If we could not place everything, the remainder is in a cycle
  if (order.length !== tasks.length) {
    const stuck = tasks.filter((t) => !order.includes(t));
    throw new Error(`Circular dependency involving: ${stuck.join(', ')}`);
  }

  return order;
}
```

**Time O(V + E), space O(V + E).**

---

## The intuition

> Repeatedly take any task whose dependencies are all satisfied, run it, and mark it done — which may unblock others.

That is exactly how a build system, a CI pipeline, or `npm install` decides what to do next.

**Cycle detection comes free:** if you run out of zero-in-degree nodes while tasks remain, those tasks depend on each other and no valid order exists. Reporting *which* tasks are involved makes the error actionable.

