## The DFS version

```js
function taskOrder(tasks, graph) {
  const visited = new Set(), inStack = new Set(), order = [];

  function dfs(task) {
    if (inStack.has(task)) throw new Error(`Cycle at ${task}`);
    if (visited.has(task)) return;

    inStack.add(task);
    for (const dep of graph.get(task) ?? []) dfs(dep);
    inStack.delete(task);

    visited.add(task);
    order.push(task);       // pushed AFTER all dependencies → correct order
  }

  tasks.forEach(dfs);
  return order;
}
```

Here the edges point from a task to its dependencies, and pushing after the recursion means dependencies land first. Note this yields a valid order, and there may be many valid orders.

